# ~/.config/switchboard/runner.env — this machine's night-shift configuration.
#
# Copy this file next to itself and edit the copy:
#
#     cp ~/.config/switchboard/runner.env.example ~/.config/switchboard/runner.env
#
# The copy is a real, machine-owned file in $HOME and is never in the dotfiles
# repository — same shape, and same reason, as ~/.config/fish/conf.d/00-local.fish.
#
# `~/bin/switchboard-runner` sources this file every tick, and the LaunchAgent
# picks up an edit on the next minute: nothing needs reloading.
#
# Precedence is one rule: **the exported environment wins, then this file, then
# the login keychain.** So a value exported by hand for a rehearsal beats this
# file, and this file beats the keychain.
#
# POSIX sh: this is sourced by bash, so no fish syntax. `export` is optional —
# the wrapper exports the names below either way.

# Where the Ward workspace is. The runner does `git pull --ff-only`,
# `ward repo refresh`, `ward session open` and `git push` here, so it must be
# clean and pushable without a passphrase prompt (the 1Password SSH agent).
# Leave unset to accept the runner's own default, ~/w/main.
#export WARD_WORKSPACE="$HOME/w/main"

# Which dayone checkout `d1` operates on. Without it `d1` walks up from the
# working directory and then falls back to whichever checkout `mise run link`
# last pointed at — and launchd has no useful working directory, so say it.
#export DAYONE_HOME="$HOME/src/dayone"

# Where the ff2k checkout lives on this machine. The `ff2k` job runs
# `/ff2k-now` with this directory as the working directory, so Claude Code
# finds ff2k's own `.claude/skills` natively. Read by `d1 switchboard pull`
# (the checkout-aware runner); harmless until that lands.
#export SWITCHBOARD_CHECKOUT_FF2K="$HOME/src/ff2k"

# The Switchboard Worker's origin. Optional here: if it is not set, the wrapper
# reads it from the login keychain instead.
#export SWITCHBOARD_URL="https://switchboard.example.workers.dev"

# SWITCHBOARD_TOKEN_MAC is deliberately NOT settable here. It is the bearer
# token the Worker authenticates this machine with, it lives in the login
# keychain, and a token does not belong in a file:
#
#     security add-generic-password -T /usr/bin/security -a "$USER" \
#       -s SWITCHBOARD_TOKEN_MAC -w '<value from the Day One vault>'
#
# `-T /usr/bin/security` is not optional in practice: without it the keychain
# asks you to approve each read, and a LaunchAgent reads once a minute.
#
# Read it back with:
#
#     security find-generic-password -a "$USER" -s SWITCHBOARD_TOKEN_MAC -w
